  "title": "Persistence needs forgetting",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "On a finite network of rings with shared contacts, under any deterministic law read from an open first row (A30, A32), an injective step has finite order p, and at every lag kp every contact's value is its own first value, which no ring's own cell decides, so no contact persists. Hence in a finite network with a persistent contact no row after the first is open (extending REBASE-KNOTS-20261109-001A to every rule and law), and no finite substrate has both an invertible step and a persistent contact (B39). Checked on 256 linear substrates, first and second order, on five tubes; reconciled with DOUBLE-BALANCE-20261109-001A, whose invertible map is the own cells' evolution, not the step.",
